WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.269
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy Award-winning actor

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.505
you know from films
such as "Inception," "Snowden,"

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.307
and "The Trial
of the Chicago 7."

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:10.042
He created, directed,
and stars in "Mr. Corman."

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:12.912
New episodes are released
Fridays on Apple TV+.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.279
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.279 --> 00:00:15.715
-I love Costco hummus.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:16.983
-Josh.
-What?

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.417
[ Chuckling ] I do.

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:21.521
[ Video-game gunfire ]

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.723
Do you see how much time
they let him have on that thing?

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:25.191
It's not okay.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:26.559
You see his eyes?

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:27.994
It's scary.

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:30.997
-You played just as many video
games when you were his age.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:34.433
I mean, for hours, you would sit
in front of that screen.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:35.701
And you turned out fine.

00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:37.570
-Did I?
-Are you done?

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:38.637
-What? I'm just
concerned for him.

00:00:38.637 --> 00:00:39.939
-No, you are
not concerned for him.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.708
-What makes you say that?
-You've always had something

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:42.809
against David.
-I don't have anything

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.176
against David.
He's just a bad person.

00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:45.478
-He's 7!

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.513
-Yeah, but you can be
a bad person when you're 7.

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:49.448
Doesn't mean
you'll stay a bad person.

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:51.484
-Please welcome back to the show
Joseph Gordon-Levitt.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:52.619
How are you, Joseph?

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:54.887
-Hello. I'm well.
Good to see you, Seth.

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:57.423
-Thank you for joining us
from halfway around the world.

00:00:57.423 --> 00:00:58.657
You're in New Zealand right now,

00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:00.994
and you have been
for a better part of this year.

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:03.763
-Yeah. Very grateful to be here.
I feel like I won the lottery

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:04.997
getting to be here
in New Zealand.

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:07.666
If you don't know,
they handled the virus here.

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:09.803
It's a huge testament
to the people of this country.

00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:11.837
They're team players, the Kiwis.

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:12.905
-I have heard from a few people

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.107
who've been lucky enough
to be there,

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.341
and that seems to be the report.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.242
I know you have young kids
with you, as well.

00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:19.311
Are they enjoying it?

00:01:19.311 --> 00:01:21.648
-Oh, yeah. I mean, I get to take
my boy to school every day.

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:24.517
Like I said, it's so,
so lucky to be here.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.619
You know, my work
got to bring me here,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:30.190
and my whole family
is just grateful every day.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.124
-So, this is a really cool show.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:35.528
You created it, directed,
star in it,

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:37.630
and very personal as well.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.800
What was the sort of beginnings
of this idea?

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.268
-Well, so, "Mr. Corman,"

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:45.271
it's about a person
who has a lot to be grateful for

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:48.774
and, you know,
who's doing his best to be happy

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:52.145
but just isn't happy
all the time.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:53.713
And, you know,
in the second episode,

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.715
he has a full-blown
anxiety attack.

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:58.417
So it's a lighthearted comedy.

00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:01.153
And, you know, there's --

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:03.690
There's a lot of talk nowadays
about mental health,

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.325
which I think is really a good
thing.

00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:07.860
There's historically
been a stigma.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.330
People going through anxiety
don't want to talk about it,

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.897
'cause they think
people will say,

00:02:11.897 --> 00:02:13.966
"Hey, you know, suck it up.
Quit whining," et cetera.

00:02:13.966 --> 00:02:17.436
But the truth is, like
one out of every six Americans

00:02:17.436 --> 00:02:19.706
has some kind
of anxiety disorder.

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:21.774
And I think it's great
and it's time

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:24.309
for all of us to be there
for each other

00:02:24.309 --> 00:02:28.347
and not telling people, like,
"Hey, just quit whining."

00:02:28.347 --> 00:02:32.184
But we're here and, you know,
offer our kindness.

00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:34.287
-You know, it's interesting,
'cause I think you're right,

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:37.290
that it is destigmatized
a great deal

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.825
compared to maybe 10 years ago.
But then you also see

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:42.361
what Simone Biles
and Naomi Osaka go through

00:02:42.361 --> 00:02:43.729
where I feel like

00:02:43.729 --> 00:02:45.965
there's great amount of empathy
from some people

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:49.035
and then a lack of understanding
and compassion on others.

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:50.936
Do you feel like
when you lay out a full story

00:02:50.936 --> 00:02:52.538
like you're given
the time to do,

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:54.740
as opposed to when
we just see, you know,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.376
people talk about mental health
in small snapshots,

00:02:57.376 --> 00:02:59.779
is the idea here that you'll
give people a better sense

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:00.947
of what that journey is like

00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:02.481
when they're dealing
with depression?

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:03.616
-I love that question.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.184
Yeah, 'cause this is
10 episodes, right?

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:08.320
So at the beginning, he's --
He is really struggling.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.491
By the end, I think he's made
some really important progress.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.526
And that can be so uplifting

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.162
when you've lived through it for
quite a while with a character

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:18.164
over these 10
half-hour episodes.

00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:20.533
-Another personal element
of this is I read

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.735
you based it on a couple friends
of yours growing up.

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:24.837
Is this something
that they were excited about,

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:27.173
or did you even
tell them the news?

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:29.742
-Oh, I told them. Yeah. A couple
of my friends are in it.

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:31.778
You know, these are guys
that aren't actors,

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:33.178
just guys I went to high school
with

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:35.180
but I managed to, like,
find little parts for them.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.149
And yeah, the part
that Arturo Castro plays,

00:03:37.149 --> 00:03:39.218
who's a brilliant actor,
was sort of inspired

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:41.321
by my friend Hector
who I went to high school with

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.456
and who actually, you know,
does work at UPS.

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:46.058
And we got Hector
into the pilot. He's in there.

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:48.794
Hector Hernandez, rocking it.
He was amazing

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:50.796
in "A Funny Thing Happened
On The Way To The Forum"

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:52.931
at Van Nuys High
when we were seniors,

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:56.469
and now, he's, you know,
on a show on Apple.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:58.437
-Was he --
Did he have any nerves about it,

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:01.507
or did he know
he had it well under control?

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.209
-Oh, hell yeah,
he was very nervous.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.544
[ Laughing ] The whole time.
But I was like,

00:04:05.544 --> 00:04:07.279
"Dude, you're going to be
totally, totally good."

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:08.715
And we got it, man.

00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:11.250
And he's totally keeping up with
like all the other four of us

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.153
who are, you know,
people who do this for a living.

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.689
And but he nailed it.
He's great in it.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:19.659
-There are, of course,
no small parts,

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:21.126
only small actors.

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:23.228
And you have taken this
to heart,

00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:25.330
because, so, Rian Johnson
is a fantastic director

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.166
who you worked with a lot

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:29.501
as sort of the lead
in films of his.

00:04:29.501 --> 00:04:32.171
I did not realize that you have
also found your way

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.040
into every one of his projects
over the years,

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.543
from "Knives Out"
to the "Star Wars" films.

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:40.946
-It's, yeah, we have a streak
going I hope we never break.

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:43.583
And yes, if I don't get
to be in one of his movies,

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:46.452
he gets me to do
these little cameos.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:49.655
I got to do a creature voice
in "The Last Jedi,"

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:51.857
and I got to do
the voice of, you know,

00:04:51.857 --> 00:04:53.826
the sort of cheesy crime drama

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:55.928
that the protagonist
happens to be watching,

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:58.130
and I'm in the background
going like,

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:01.867
"You can't handle the truth!"
or whatever it was that I said.

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:03.903
-It's very nice
to have a relationship

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:07.974
with a director like that that
can survive over multiple films.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.542
-I'm very lucky, yeah.
Rian's amazing. It's true.

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:12.844
-You play a fifth grade teacher
in this show.

00:05:12.844 --> 00:05:15.147
A lot of people always ask me,
"If you weren't doing

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:17.750
what you're doing,
what job would you want?"

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.620
I've always sort of thought
teacher might be a path.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.622
I'm lucky enough to be the son
of a public school teacher.

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:25.457
What was --
Is teaching something

00:05:25.457 --> 00:05:27.826
that you could have
seen yourself doing?

00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:30.496
-Okay, well, first of all, is it
your mom or dad who's a teacher?

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:31.730
-My mom.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.799
-Okay. So round of applause
for your mom

00:05:33.799 --> 00:05:35.535
and huge, huge respect.

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:36.935
I think teachers are heroes.

00:05:36.935 --> 00:05:39.372
And I've always had
this same feeling

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:41.106
that I've wanted to be a teacher

00:05:41.106 --> 00:05:43.008
and would love to do that
at some point in my life.

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:45.077
That's why I wrote this
character to be a teacher.

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:49.716
If I could wave a magic wand,
I would have us live in a world

00:05:49.716 --> 00:05:52.452
where teachers were the ones
that we were all celebrating,

00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:54.253
and we were all chatting
on social media

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:56.723
about, like, "Oh, did you see
that new curriculum

00:05:56.723 --> 00:05:58.324
from that one teacher?
It's so amazing!"

00:05:58.324 --> 00:06:01.860
Like, we would live
in such a better place

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.630
if teachers were all making
hundreds of thousands

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.199
or millions of dollars a year.

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:07.866
I think
that should be the new rule.

00:06:07.866 --> 00:06:11.104
I think like the bottom salary,
yearly salary,

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:14.440
for a teacher
should be quarter mil.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:16.608
And then everyone,
the best and the brightest

00:06:16.608 --> 00:06:18.511
would competing
for teacher jobs.

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.112
Like, that would be way better

00:06:20.112 --> 00:06:21.813
than people competing
for acting jobs.

00:06:21.813 --> 00:06:25.851
[ Laughing ] What do actors
really offer the world?

00:06:25.851 --> 00:06:29.221
-Plus, I mean, I think people
don't quite realize

00:06:29.221 --> 00:06:31.758
how lucky you are if you have
a parent who's a teacher,

00:06:31.758 --> 00:06:33.893
because they have access
to a laminator.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.595
And...
-Mm.

00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:37.597
-You know, that's a giant
piece of equipment.

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.498
Like, no one has a laminator
in their home.

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:42.835
But you get someone in a public
school system, they can go in,

00:06:42.835 --> 00:06:44.604
and they could laminate
that comic book for you.

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:46.873
-How big a thing
can you laminate,

00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:48.641
like just a standard paper?

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:49.842
Can you, like, laminate
a bigger --

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:52.111
-I feel like I use --
You can go bigger than --

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:55.614
I felt like I laminated a lot of
covers of "Sports Illustrated."

00:06:55.614 --> 00:06:58.217
That was like a thing that
I would do growing up, so...

00:06:58.217 --> 00:06:59.652
-'Cause what I would want to do

00:06:59.652 --> 00:07:02.755
would be laminate the biggest
thing I could possibly laminate,

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:05.057
because what I love
about laminated things

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:06.358
is you can like [Warbling]

00:07:06.358 --> 00:07:08.394
They make that sound
when you shake them.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:10.796
You know what I mean?
-You're right. You have a far --

00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:13.866
You have a better vision
for that opportunity than I had.

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:16.368
I'm already looking back
and regretting my past.

00:07:16.368 --> 00:07:17.870
[ Both laugh ]

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.605
Hey, congratulations
on the show.

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:20.773
Thanks for making time
for us today.

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:22.240
It's always great to see you.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:23.342
-So good to see you, man.

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:24.777
Thank you.
-Be well.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:27.380
New episodes of "Mr. Corman" are
released Fridays on Apple TV+.

